   "content": {
    "text": "<em>Listen and subscribe to the podcast «NZZ Akzent» on <a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/show/4uoTZbK8rBcVAzAk5f7tIr?si=Qog1qTM4ROaG-fzLBxIgNA\">Spotify,</a> <a href=\"https://podcasts.apple.com/ch/podcast/nzz-akzent/id1506761994\">Apple Podcasts,</a> or <a href=\"https://castbox.fm/channel/NZZ-Akzent-id2982237\">Castbox.</a></em><a href=\"https://podcasts.apple.com/ch/podcast/nzz-corona-podcast/id1506761994\"></a>"
   },
   "data": {},
   "paidContent": true,
   "prevElement": "barrier",
   "nextElement": "subtitle"
  },
  {
   "identifier": "subtitle",
   "id": "doc-1e31r87h90",
   "content": {
    "title": "In this podcast episode",
    "id": "subtitle-in-dieser-podcast-episode"
   },
   "contentTable": false,
   "paidContent": true,
   "prevElement": "p-text",
   "nextElement": "p-text"
  },
  {
   "identifier": "p-text",
   "id": "doc-1k11pnmlb1",
   "content": {
    "text": "It began like any normal day for Sergeant Earl Lundy. The paratrooper was preparing food at the U.S. base in Afghanistan. Suddenly there was a white light, then darkness. Lundy found himself buried under meters of rubble. His leg was trapped under a steel beam. Doctors would later tell him he would never walk again. A suicide bomber had blown himself up, and two of his comrades died."
   },
   "paidContent": true,
   "prevElement": "subtitle",
   "nextElement": "p-text"
  },
  {
   "identifier": "p-text",
   "id": "doc-1k1edb0j21",
   "content": {
    "text": "There are thousands of stories like Earl Lundy's. 25 years after the September 11 attacks, troops have withdrawn from Afghanistan and Iraq, but the wars continue to rage in the minds of the soldiers. Official sources indicate that over 40% of veterans suffer from post-traumatic stress disorder, depression, or addiction. However, the state system usually leaves them to fend for themselves after their wartime deployment."
   },
   "paidContent": true,
   "prevElement": "p-text",
   "nextElement": "p-text"
  },
  {
   "identifier": "p-text",
   "id": "doc-1k1ea8gnm0",
   "content": {
    "text": "U.S. correspondent Andreas Scheiner spoke with several veterans who went to war for the U.S. after 9/11. In the podcast «NZZ Akzent,» he reports on these encounters and his visit to the «Warriors Heart Ranch,» where traumatized veterans try to put their lives back together."
   },
   "data": {},
   "paidContent": true,
   "prevElement": "p-text",
   "nextElement": "p-text"
  },
  {
